[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the application service(s) on srv1 (the Office B “campus server”) are not running/listening; the network is healthy. 

Mechanism: From PC1 we can ping srv1 (10.5.0.4) with 0% loss and low RTT, proving end-to-end IP reachability. However, application connections fail because no server process is bound to the expected ports. A curl test from PC1 to http://10.5.0.4 returns HTTP code 000 immediately, indicating no listener rather than a network drop. On srv1, ss -ltnp shows only named (BIND) listening on 127.0.0.1:53/953 and 10.5.0.4:53; there is no listener on common service ports such as 80/443 (web), 445/139 (SMB), or 22 (SSH). iptables -S shows default ACCEPT policies, so a local firewall is not blocking traffic.

Localization: srv1 (interface eth0, 10.5.0.4/24) at the process/service layer. The distribution/core path between Office A and B is fine (pc1↔pc3 ping OK; pc1↔srv1 ping OK).

Root cause: A change last night stopped/disabled or removed the application service(s) on srv1, leaving only DNS running.

Corrective fix: Restore the intended service on srv1 and enable it at boot. For example, if it’s a web service: install/restore configuration, then systemctl enable --now nginx (or apache2) and verify with ss -ltnp that 10.5.0.4:80/443 are LISTENING. If it’s file sharing: install/restore Samba, systemctl enable --now smbd nmbd and verify ports 445/139. Re-test from PC1. Also revert the prior change or apply configuration management to prevent recurrence.
=== END ===